  "name": "SUGAR RANDOM URINE",
  "code": "URINE-SUGAR-RND",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Test</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Normal Result</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Abnormal Result</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Urine Sugar (Random sample)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Not detected / Negative</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Positive (Glucosuria)</strong></td>\n<td>- <strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong> (most common cause) - Renal glycosuria (defect in renal tubular reabsorption of glucose despite normal blood glucose) - Endocrine disorders (Cushing&rsquo;s syndrome, thyrotoxicosis, acromegaly) - Pregnancy (may show mild glucosuria due to increased GFR) - After high carbohydrate intake (&ldquo;alimentary glycosuria&rdquo;)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"731\" data-end=\"825\">\n<p data-start=\"733\" data-end=\"825\">Normally, glucose is completely reabsorbed by renal tubules, so urine is glucose-negative.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"826\" data-end=\"926\">\n<p data-start=\"828\" data-end=\"926\"><strong data-start=\"828\" data-end=\"884\">Renal threshold for glucose ~180 mg/dL blood glucose</strong>. Above this, glucose spills into urine.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"927\" data-end=\"1052\">\n<p data-start=\"929\" data-end=\"1052\">Positive urine sugar does <strong data-start=\"955\" data-end=\"979\">not confirm diabetes</strong>&mdash;it should be correlated with <strong data-start=\"1009\" data-end=\"1035\">blood glucose or HbA1c</strong> for diagnosis.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1053\" data-end=\"1137\">\n<p data-start=\"1055\" data-end=\"1137\">Trace amounts in pregnancy or after large carbohydrate meals may be physiologic.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
